            <p class="journal-story-product-category">
              CERAMICS
            </p>

            <h3>
              Botanical Vintage Pitcher
            </h3>

            <p class="journal-story-product-brand">
              Ethereal Eves
            </p>

            <a
              href="/kasodi-edit/botanical-vintage-pitcher"
              class="journal-story-discover"
            >
              Discover &rarr;
            </a>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- 07 -->

      <section class="journal-story-row journal-story-row-product journal-story-divider">

        <div class="journal-inline-product">

          <a href="/kitchen/nukha-acacia-toast-rack">
            <img
              src="/images/kitchen/kave-home-nukha-acacia-toast-rack.png"
              alt="Nukha Acacia Toast Rack by Kave Home"
            />
          </a>

          <div class="journal-inline-product-info">

            <p class="journal-story-product-category">
              SERVING &amp; BREAKFAST
            </p>

            <h3>
              Nukha Acacia Toast Rack
            </h3>

            <p class="journal-story-product-brand">
              Kave Home
            </p>

            <a
              href="/kitchen/nukha-acacia-toast-rack"
              class="journal-story-discover"
            >
              Discover &rarr;
            </a>

          </div>

        </div>


        <div class="journal-story-copy">

          <p class="journal-story-number">
            07
          </p>

          <h2>
            Keep a Few Sculptural Objects
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every useful object needs to disappear into a cupboard once you've
            finished using it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A marble mortar has weight and presence. A toast rack brings rhythm through
            its repeated curves. A wooden spatula can add a simple vertical line to a
            ceramic utensil pot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at them differently and they start to feel almost sculptural &mdash;
            objects with a purpose, but also a shape worth noticing.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is where styling becomes less about adding decoration and more about
            paying attention to the things already around us.
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- 08 + CLOSING -->

      <section class="journal-story-closing">

        <div class="journal-story-ending">

          <p class="journal-story-number">
            08
          </p>

          <h2>
            Leave Some Space
          </h2>

          <p>
            Perhaps the most important part of styling a kitchen is knowing what to
            leave out. Empty space gives the objects around it more presence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Instead of filling the entire counter, choose a few areas where things
            naturally belong and let the rest remain quiet.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same applies to open shelving. A stack of plates, a glass vessel and
            a small piece of art can often feel more thoughtful than a shelf filled
            from one end to the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            A calm kitchen isn't necessarily an empty one. It's a kitchen where each
            object has enough space to be seen.
          </p>

        </div>


        <aside class="journal-story-note">

          <p class="product-detail-eyebrow">
            KASODI NOTE
          </p>

          <h3>
            A beautiful kitchen doesn't need more things. It needs the right things,
            given enough space to be noticed.
          </h3>

        </aside>

      </section>


      <!-- EVERYDAY RITUALS -->

      <section class="journal-story-row journal-story-divider">

        <div class="journal-story-copy">

          <p class="product-detail-eyebrow">
            EVERYDAY RITUALS
          </p>

          <h2>
            Make Everyday Rituals Part of the Design
          </h2>

          <p>
            Toast in a wooden rack. Fresh herbs crushed in a marble mortar. Water
            poured from a beautiful carafe. A serving tray carried outside on a warm
            evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            These are small moments, but they're also the moments when a home is
            actually lived in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choosing objects that make ordinary routines a little more enjoyable
            isn't about creating a perfect kitchen. It's about having things around
            you that feel good to see, touch and use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Perhaps that is the real art of styling a kitchen: not decorating the
            room around everyday life, but allowing everyday life to become part of
            the room.
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- PIECES FOR THE KITCHEN -->

      <section class="journal-look-edit">

        <div class="journal-look-heading">

          <p class="product-detail-eyebrow">
            KASODI EDIT
          </p>

          <h2>
            Pieces for the Kitchen
          </h2>

          <p>
            Useful objects chosen for their materials, character and ability to
            become part of the room.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="journal-look-grid">


          <article class="journal-look-card">

            <a
              href="/kitchen/cindea-green-marble-mortar-pestle"
              class="journal-look-image"
